<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#ukiyo-e">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ukiyo-e</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ukiyo-e, meaning 'pictures of the floating world', is a genre of Japanese woodblock prints and paintings that flourished from the seventeenth to the nineteenth centuries. It depicted actors, courtesans, landscapes and scenes of urban life. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> count it among the cultural achievements of the period, noting its later influence abroad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ukiyo-e.jpg" alt="Ukiyo-e" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sharaku · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Toshusai_Sharaku-_Otani_Oniji,_1794.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
